{
  "dom_id": "step-q148367",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$",
